title The door's first duty -- Pond's record can say who walks through it, and a scan stopped running a build it did not make
prompt The HUSH seat prompt: roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, rota row N mod 5 advancing by hand past a repeat, QA on touch, read scope, ListAgents before a file moves; pick up the running thread and take the next Pond lap under the accrete-only gate boundary.
think THE PEER, NAMED BEFORE A FILE MOVED. ListAgents answered no reachable agents, so ps carried the naming again -- and this time it arrived by accident, while I was checking why my own roster had printed nothing. pgrep found TWO runners: pid 15962 running --hot out of /home/keeper/grain-dream, and pid 4142854 running cold out of /home/keeper/grain. Dream on its own checkout, me on mine, my index clean throughout. The one-tree-per-star arrangement holds for a third lap running.
think ROSTER COLD, FORTY MINUTES, AND THE TREE HELD STILL. tree_at_open and tree_at_close both a41678d68df2, tree_moved=no, host=linux, skipped_host=0. 93 guards, 90 green, 3 red. I read the whole thing before touching a byte, which is what the directive asks and what the runner enforces from the other side.
think THE THREE REDS, ATTRIBUTED RATHER THAN LUMPED. rule_twin at 39 over 36 is custody gate %7 and Keaton's by name. width_check is the corpus ratchet standing at 331/329 and 1265/1263, two over each, and the Silence lap of 12:47 already named its two causes -- another body's. remember_git_nib is MINE and closes with this send: the card's nib read 8dc023d68c against HEAD ce2a89165c, which is the one red a work commit repairs by carrying the pin inside it. And witness_reach, which I surfaced as a red last lap and left for Mind, reads GREEN -- the Mac bench's own fix landed.
think ROTA. N=3733 by git rev-list --count HEAD gives row 3, WATER -- deep-read three separate times today already (09:42, and twice more by other bodies). The last row actually read was 4, EARTH, on my own 12:35 lap. So I advanced by hand to row 0, AETHER, which is the only row today had not opened: the threshold page, then follow-our-compass, the Lindy effect, and the grain and the crossing. What the row asks is why the work exists, and its sense is sound -- listen for what the work is saying under the words. Two of its sentences chose this lap's shape: a guard that cannot red guards nothing, and favorite words over fashion words.
think REDS FIRST, AND NOTHING IN MY LANE WAS CLOSABLE. Nine rows stand OPEN. %327 is Pond's and rests on this pier's own enclosure.conf, which is Keaton's hand. %306's remaining direction is a seat. %301 is five booked folds. %311 is Sound's language custody. %326, %328, %330 are seats or booked design laps. %291 is gated. So I took the door my own prior recommend and the card both name: orbit four.
think THE DESIGN SAID user FIRST, AND SAID IT AS A PROPOSAL. active-designing/20260829-064107 counts the three duties the record's grammar cannot express -- entry, env, user -- and orders them: user is cheapest and most valuable, one key and one integer; env next; entry last, because naming the program is the switchover and the switchover is Keaton's word. It then says plainly that the ordering is a proposal.
think AND THE MEASUREMENT REFUTED THE INTEGER. Three readings on this pier, and they are the same three that refuted `network off` one mark over: tools/ag/agent-jail.sh:382's exec line passes no uid flag to ai-jail; `ai-jail --help` offers no uid or user option at all, only --systemd-user and a Docker note; and the door guard's own probe read the agent running at uid 1000, the invoking user's. A number in that record would be a claim nothing keeps, which is REDS %329 exactly. So the record declares what the launcher DOES -- `user invoking` -- and the design's own first step lands with its value corrected by the tree.
think THE FIVE BRANCHES, EACH GROUNDED RATHER THAN CHOSEN. `invoking` places, because it is what happens. `root` refuses BY NAME, because tools/ag/agent-jail.sh:303-305 already tells an operator the opposite in its own words -- claude refuses --dangerously-skip-permissions at uid 0, so run the jail as a non-root user; that note is the comment somebody hopes is read, and this line is the thing a program checks. `0`, `00`, `000` refuse with it, since root by number is root by name. A bare non-zero uid HOLDS on verdict_for_network's own reasoning: no flag could set it, so an operator may want it and should say so out loud, and the roster stays open for a Pond enclosure that genuinely sets one. Everything else refuses.
think A PLACEHOLDER REFUSES, AND THAT IS THE ONE PLACE THIS MARK PARTS FROM THE PATH MARKS. The record writes `<uid>` inside an rw-map path and that line still places, because a path is judged on structure and a placeholder keeps the structure. In a `user` line the value IS the whole claim, so a placeholder leaves nothing to check -- which is precisely the unchecked comment the mark exists to replace.
obs THE SCAN PROVED A BUILD THAT NO LONGER EXISTED, AND ITS OWN FIRST RUN CAUGHT IT. tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_policy_scan.sh read `if ! test -x "$BIN"`, so a binary built once stood forever. My new mark came back REFUSED as an unknown token, and -- this is the part that matters -- the SELFTEST leg one line above had just printed ok, on those same stale bytes. A guard that runs a build it did not make cannot tell you the source moved. That is REDS %334's family one step on: there a witness named its build in a comment and ran the binary, here a scan made a build and never remade it. It owns its build every run now, measured: 1.4s for the whole scan from a deleted binary, of which 0.8s is the build.
obs A NUMBER THAT BECAME TRUE BY ACCIDENT, NAMED RATHER THAN POCKETED. max_declarations' comment claimed the record declares thirty-two, counted `20260829`. HEAD's record carries THIRTY-ONE -- I counted both, before and after. Seating `user` makes thirty-two true. The comment now reads correct for a reason nobody measured, so the correction is written into it out loud, along with the second slip it carried: it listed `format` among what the parser skips and omitted `name`, which the parser also skips.
obs THE SETTLING, PROVEN FROM BOTH SIDES ON METAL. A duty the record can state is a duty something must check, or the key is a comment with a colon in it. The derived leg lifts the declaration; the probe leg settles it against a running kernel -- `invoking` against the uid running the scan, a fixed uid against itself. On the living record: uid 1000, which agrees, door_disagreements=0. Against a record planted with `user 65534` in a pen outside the tree: door_disagreements=1, verdict=door_disagrees. The witness runs that second leg where ai-jail stands and SAYS SO PLAINLY where it cannot, rather than skipping in silence (%311).
obs THE CONTROL WAS ALREADY SOUND WHERE I EXPECTED TO REPAIR IT. I opened the lap expecting to fix duties_absent, which asserts duties_undeclared=3 -- a number my change to the live record would make stale. It does not: new_pen plants its OWN record from a heredoc rather than copying the tree's, so that 3 is the control's own construction and stays true whatever the living record grows. That is the discipline I wrote into pond_seal_gate's control yesterday, already standing here, and finding it took one read rather than one repair.
obs SIX NEW CONTROL CASES, ALL DERIVED, ALL JAIL-FREE. 57 behaviors now, zero failures. The record's declaration is proven READ from three shapes -- absent (user_declared=unstated), the word (invoking), and a fixed uid (1000) -- in pens with no enclosure anywhere, which is the state of every clone this guard works on. The SETTLING is what needs a kernel, and the control's header now says which half lives where instead of leaving a reader to work it out.
obs THE GAP PIN IS DELIBERATE. duties_undeclared falls 3 -> 2 and the witness pins the 2, on pond_enclosure_policy_scan.sh's own stated reasoning about its place/hold/refuse counts: a line that quietly stops being counted moves the number, and so does the next duty seated. The lap that seats `env` moves this pin in the same breath, on purpose. That pin caught my own change today, one file over, which is the argument for it.
obs QA ON TOUCH, SIX ARTIFACTS AT METER: pond/enclosure_policy.rye, pond/enclosure_policy.kyri, both scans, the control, and the witness. Every one composite 94, letter A, truth 100 with all cited paths resolving. Service judged 75 rather than handed a number, for last lap's reason: named_by_card reads the guard's name and not its paths. No frame pushed.
obs THE CARD HAD ONE BYTE OF SLACK, AND THE FETCH HANDED ME THE ROOM BACK. construction/ITINERARY.md stood at 24,575 of its 24,576 bound when I opened it, so every byte this lap added had to be paid for by one removed; five passes of tightening brought my Now block from 2,060 to 1,637 and the card to 24,565. Then `git fetch xy` arrived with four commits, one of them the Mac bench condensing that same card 24,575 -> 22,762 at 14:22 -- it had booked and been granted exactly this squeeze on its own aether lap, twenty minutes before mine. So I dropped my edits rather than force them through a conflict, fast-forwarded, and re-applied the block onto the card that exists: 23,571 with a thousand bytes to spare. The tightening was not wasted, since a shorter block is the right block either way, and the sequence is the argument for editing the tree you stand on rather than the one you read.
obs I FABRICATED TWO STAMPS AND THE CLOCK CORRECTED THEM. I wrote 20260829.152000 into the module's version string and into the card's prior-lap row while drafting, before reading the clock. The clock says 143651. Both are corrected and the module rebuilt; the live-clock rule exists because a stamp guessed forward is a stamp that sorts wrong forever.
obs WHAT THE ACCRETE-ONLY BOUNDARY DID NOT COST ME. No markdown that instructs or references ai-jail moved. The ENCLOSURE selector still reads ai-jail. The launcher is READ -- its exec line, its uid warning, its search path -- and rewritten nowhere. A new mark in Pond's own grammar is pure accretion, and a mark once published keeps its meaning.
obs THE SEND MET A MOVED REMOTE AND TOOK THE LONGER ROAD ON PURPOSE. Upstream's four commits touched two files I had also touched -- the card and today's index shelf. With no local commits yet, a stash, a fast-forward, and a re-apply beat a rebase through a conflict on a card another body had just rewritten. The index resolved by taking upstream's forty rows whole and prepending mine below the delimiter, so no peer's row was lost to my resolution. Upstream also carries a new `pier/diverged-20260829-142504` branch, which is the sync rota parking a package after three refusals rather than forcing.

obs A PARTIAL ANSWER TO THE DOOR I COULD NOT MEASURE, WHICH IS BETTER THAN THE HYPOTHESIS IT REPLACES. The card asks whether the Mac's three red Pond rows want `host linux`. I cannot start a macOS run from here, so I read the tracked bytes instead: tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_door_scan.sh:158 uses `xargs -r`, a GNU extension BSD xargs refuses outright. That is a portability fault rather than a host boundary, so at least one of the three wants a REPAIR and marking it `host linux` would blind the Mac to a reading it could take. Naming it as one measured line beats carrying the whole question as a guess.
obs DECLINED TO BOOK A REDS ROW, FOR THE FOURTH LAP RUNNING AND THE SAME MEASURED REASON. construction/REDS.md holds 24,571 of its 24,576 bytes with zero foldable rows, and %338 is booked and Keaton's. The stale-build finding's lesson is written into the scan's own header where the next reader of that instrument meets it, which is where it does the work.
