scope Pond's enclosure grammar -- orbit four's first door duty, seated and settled against a kernel
status landed GREEN on metal; eight Pond guards green, TAME style green, width unchanged and another body's
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- POND'S RECORD CAN NOW SAY WHO WALKS THROUGH ITS DOOR. Every mark in the grammar described the ROOM -- what an agent inside can see, write, or reach. `user` is the first of the three duties that describe the DOOR, and orbit four's own reading named it first as one key and one integer. THE INTEGER IS WHAT THE TREE CORRECTED: the launcher's exec line passes no uid flag, `ai-jail --help` offers no uid option at all, and the probe read the agent at uid 1000 -- so a number in that record would be a claim nothing keeps, which is REDS %329 one mark over. It declares `user invoking`, which is what the launcher does, and `root` refuses by name because the launcher's own note already tells an operator to run as a non-root user. That note was a comment somebody hopes is read; it is a thing a program checks now. `duties_undeclared` falls 3 -> 2, and the claim is SETTLED rather than stated: agreeing against a running kernel on the living record, refusing on a planted uid, both on metal. MY OWN GUARD WAS PROVING A BUILD THAT NO LONGER EXISTED, and its first run caught it: the policy scan built its binary once and reran it forever, so my new mark came back as an unknown token while the SELFTEST leg above printed ok on the same stale bytes. That is %334's family one step on -- there a witness named its build in a comment, here a scan made one and never remade it. It owns its build now, 0.8s of a 1.4s scan. A NUMBER BECAME TRUE BY ACCIDENT AND I NAMED IT: `max_declarations`' comment claimed thirty-two while the record carried thirty-one, and seating `user` made it right for a reason nobody measured. THE ONE DOOR I COULD NOT MEASURE, ANSWERED IN PART: your card asks whether the Mac's three red Pond rows want `host linux`. `xargs -r` at pond_enclosure_door_scan.sh:158 is a GNU extension BSD refuses, so at least one wants a REPAIR, and marking it `host linux` would blind that bench to a reading it could take. YOURS, UNCHANGED: `rule_twin` 39/36 is gate %7; `width_check`'s corpus at 331/329 is another body's lap; the REDS pin holds 24,571 of 24,576 with zero foldable rows, so this lap booked no row for the fourth time and wrote its lesson into the instrument's header instead. WITNESS_REACH IS GREEN -- the red I surfaced and left for Mind last lap was paid by the bench that could pay it. MINE NEXT: the `xargs -r` repair, then `env`, the second door duty, whose values the reachability rule already knows how to read.
